  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "98b17b2c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The athletes that are duplicated within a season are shown on different teams in the same season. I did some research, using Brendan Terry as an example, and found that the Southeast Missouri State players who played in their game against UNC Asheville each have a separate row for that game, where they are mislabeled as UNC Asheville players. The points in those rows match the points the players scored in that game's box score. \n",
    "\n",
    "The only exception is A.J. Lawson. He is the only athlete where one of his two rows isn't a single game. After researching, I found out this is two different A.J. Lawsons with the same `athleteId` (31449). One played for South Carolina, and the other played for McNeese. I will separate these players and give one of them a different `athleteID`.\n",
    "\n",
    "After this, for each duplicated athlete, I will keep the row with the most games, since that is the team they actually played for."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 36,
   "id": "fb00f43a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# athleteId 31449 is shared by two different players named A.J. Lawson (McNeese and South Carolina)\n",
    "# Give the South Carolina player his own ID so the two are no longer mixed up.\n",
    "new_id = max(df[\"athleteId\"].max() for df in datasets.values()) + 1 # Determine the max athleteId and add one\n",
    "\n",
    "for year, df in datasets.items(): # Give the SC player a new athleteId\n",
    "    is_sc_lawson = (df[\"athleteId\"] == 31449) & (df[\"team\"] == \"South Carolina\")\n",
    "    df.loc[is_sc_lawson, \"athleteId\"] = new_id"
   ]
  },
