    "from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\n",
    "\n",
    "k_start = round(len(X_train) ** 0.5) # Starting k based on the square root of the number of training rows\n",
    "print(f\"Square root of n: k = {k_start}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "ks = np.arange(1, 201) # Test every k from 1 to 200, which covers a wide range around the square root of n\n",
    "knn_pipeline = make_pipeline(\n",
    "    SimpleImputer(strategy=\"median\", add_indicator=True), # Fill missing values with the training median and add a column that flags which values were missing\n",
    "    StandardScaler(), # Standardize every feature so no feature dominates the distance\n",
    "    KNeighborsClassifier())\n",
    "knn_grid = {\"kneighborsclassifier__n_neighbors\": ks}\n",
    "\n",
    "knn_searches = {} # Empty dictionary to save results\n",
    "for set_name, features in feature_sets.items(): # Loop through two feature sets\n",
    "    # For every k, fit on 4 folds and score on the 5th, rotating through all 5 folds, then average\n",
    "    knn_search = GridSearchCV(knn_pipeline, knn_grid, cv=cv, scoring=scoring, refit=\"accuracy\", return_train_score=True)\n",
    "    knn_search.fit(X_train[features], y_train, groups=groups_train)\n",
    "    knn_searches[set_name] = knn_search \n",
    "\n",
    "    train_accuracy = knn_search.cv_results_[\"mean_train_accuracy\"] # Accuracy on the folds the model was fit on\n",
    "    valid_accuracy = knn_search.cv_results_[\"mean_test_accuracy\"] # Accuracy on the held-out fold\n",
    "    i = knn_search.best_index_ # Row of the k with the highest validation accuracy\n",
    "    k_star = ks[i]\n",
    "    cv_results[f\"kNN ({set_name})\"] = {\"accuracy\": valid_accuracy[i], # Save the model scores\n",
    "                                       \"log loss\": -knn_search.cv_results_[\"mean_test_neg_log_loss\"][i]}\n",
    "    print(f\"\\n{set_name}: k = {k_star}. Validation accuracy: {valid_accuracy[i]:.1%}.\")\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Accuracy for a few candidate values of k\n",
    "    candidate_ks = sorted({1, 5, 15, 50, k_start, k_star, 150, 200})\n",
    "    candidate_table = pd.DataFrame({\"Training accuracy\": [train_accuracy[k - 1] for k in candidate_ks],\n",
    "                                    \"Validation accuracy\": [valid_accuracy[k - 1] for k in candidate_ks]},\n",
    "                                   index=pd.Index(candidate_ks, name=\"k\"))\n",
    "    print((candidate_table * 100).round(1).astype(str) + \"%\")\n",
    "\n",
    "    plt.plot(ks, train_accuracy, label=\"Training\")\n",
    "    plt.plot(ks, valid_accuracy, label=\"Validation\")\n",
    "    plt.axvline(k_star, color=\"gray\", linestyle=\"--\", label=f\"Best k = {k_star}\")\n",
    "    plt.xlabel(\"k (number of neighbors)\")\n",
    "    plt.ylabel(\"Cross-validated accuracy\")\n",
    "    plt.title(f\"kNN validation accuracy peaks at k = {k_star} ({set_name})\")\n",
    "    plt.legend()\n",
    "    plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "models-knn-t",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To choose k, we first used the square root of the number of training rows as a starting point, which is k = 64. We then tested every k from 1 to 200 on both feature sets (all 37 features and our 12 selected features). For each k, we used cross-validation to fit the model 5 times, each time on 4 of the 5 folds. We then measured accuracy on the unused fold (validation accuracy) and on the folds it was fit on (training accuracy), and averaged over all 5 folds. We chose the k with the highest validation accuracy. We use accuracy instead of an error measure like SSE because our target is binary. The best k is 116 for all 37 features (validation accuracy 60.7%) and 150 for the 12 selected features (validation accuracy 61.5%).\n",
    "\n",
    "When k = 1, the model has 100% training accuracy, but it performs about the same as just guessing starter on new players (54.1% validation accuracy on all 37 features). This means that the model memorized the training data and is overfit. Validation accuracy increases as k increases, from 57.0% at k = 5 to 58.4% at k = 15 and 59.8% at k = 50. This is because using more nearest neighbors makes the prediction less sensitive to any one player. It peaks at the best k and then decreases (59.3% at k = 200 on all 37 features) due to underfitting, since averaging over too many players blurs the real differences between them. A large best k fits our data since our training data has around 4,100 players and is relatively noisy.\n",
    "\n",
    "PARAGRAPH ON WHY WE CHOSE MODEL 1"
   ]
  },
