  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "79bb3414",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We chose a random forest as our third model because it works differently from the first two. kNN predicts from the most similar players, and the lasso assumes each stat pushes the chance of starting up or down in a straight line. A random forest builds many decision trees on random samples of the players and averages their votes, so it can pick up nonlinear patterns and interactions between stats without us specifying them. Additionally, it also doesn't need the features to be scaled and is not thrown off by overlapping features, like the ones in our correlation plot. If the forest does no better than the lasso, that suggests a simple linear relationship is enough to predict starting.\n",
    "\n",
    "We tuned three settings with the same 5 grouped, stratified folds as the other models: `max_depth` (how deep each tree can grow), `min_samples_leaf` (the minimum number of players in a leaf), and `max_features` (how many features each split can consider). Deep trees with small leaves memorize the training data, like kNN with k = 1, so limiting depth and leaf size is how we control overfitting. We fixed the number of trees at 500 since more trees only make the average more stable rather than overfit. As with the other models, we fit it on both the 37 features and the 12 selected features and chose the settings with the highest validation accuracy."
   ]
