    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "ba5966f1",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ba5966f1"
      },
      "source": [
        "This horizontal bar plot shows the correlation between a prior-season feature and whether a player started more than half of his games at his new school. All of the correlations are pretty weak, and the strongest is assists per game at 0.206, then minutes per game at 0.199, defensive win shares per game at 0.184, field goal attempts per game at 0.181, points per game at 0.177, and games played at 0.176. Most of the top features measure how much a player played or how much of the offense he handled, which makes sense, since players who were on the floor and involved the most last year are more likely to start at their next school. Efficiency stats like field goal percentage, effective field goal percentage, and true shooting percentage are close to zero, so how efficiently a player scored matters much less that we would have expected. The only meaningful negative correlations are offensive rebound percentage (about -0.07), defensive rating (about -0.05), and the forward and center position groups (about -0.08 and -0.03), although these are all small."
      ]
    },
