    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "models-knn-t",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "models-knn-t"
      },
      "source": [
        "To choose k, we first used the square root of the number of training rows as a starting point, which is k = 64. We then tested every k from 1 to 200 on both feature sets (all 37 features and our 12 selected features). For each k, we used cross-validation to fit the model 5 times, each time on 4 of the 5 folds. We then measured accuracy on the unused fold (validation accuracy) and on the folds it was fit on (training accuracy), and averaged over all 5 folds. We chose the k with the highest validation accuracy. We use accuracy instead of an error measure like SSE because our target is binary. The best k is 116 for all 37 features (validation accuracy 60.7%) and 150 for the 12 selected features (validation accuracy 61.5%).\n",
        "\n",
        "When k = 1, the model has 100% training accuracy, but it performs about the same as just guessing starter on new players (54.1% validation accuracy on all 37 features). This means that the model memorized the training data and is overfit. Validation accuracy increases as k increases, from 57.0% at k = 5 to 58.4% at k = 15 and 59.8% at k = 50. This is because using more nearest neighbors makes the prediction less sensitive to any one player. It peaks at the best k and then decreases (59.3% at k = 200 on all 37 features) due to underfitting, since averaging over too many players blurs the real differences between them. A large best k fits our data since our training data has around 4,100 players and is relatively noisy.\n",
        "\n",
        "PARAGRAPH ON WHY WE CHOSE MODEL 1"
      ]
    },
