      "metadata": {
        "id": "models-lasso-t"
      },
      "source": [
        "We used the lasso penalty to shrink the weights of unhelpful features to exactly zero, similar to when we were choosing the number of features to use in our models. We standardized the features so the penalty treats them equally, and, like in the feature selection section, we chose the penalty strength with the lowest log loss from the same 20 options, since accuracy was very similar across penalties. We repeated this for each feature set. On all 37 features the best penalty is C = 0.0785, which is the same C we selected when determining the number of features, and on the 12 selected features it is C = 1.4384. The penalty is much weaker on the selected features because our feature selection step already removed the weaker features, and the lasso kept all 12.\n",
        "\n",
        "On all 37 features, the lasso has an accuracy of 61.5% and a log loss of 0.653, and it keeps 21 of the 37 features. The largest weights are on defensive win shares per game (0.190), assists per game (0.181), games played last year (0.158), and net rating (0.147), while effective field goal percentage has a negative weight (-0.142). On the 12 selected features, the accuracy is 62.3% and the log loss is 0.652. The 12 selected features has approximately an 0.8% better accuracy than using all 37 features. This is well above the baseline of 54.1%.\n",
        "\n",
        "PARAGRAPH ON MODEL 2"
      ]
